# Backend HTTP server port. Read by the backend, the Vite dev proxy and `npm run stop`.
BACKEND_PORT=4000

# Interface the backend binds to (default: 127.0.0.1, reachable only from this machine). ApiPilot is
# single-user and local-only: it has no authentication, executes user-supplied scripts and holds
# stored credentials. Leave this unset. Set it (for example 0.0.0.0) only if you accept that anyone
# who can reach the port gets that access; a non-loopback value also disables the Host/Origin
# loopback check that guards against DNS rebinding.
# BACKEND_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Short commit hash reported by GET /api/version (default: falls back to `git rev-parse
# --short HEAD` in a working git checkout, then "unknown" if that also fails — e.g. a
# deployed copy without a .git directory or without git installed). Set this from CI/CD
# when building a deployable artifact so the reported commit reflects the actual build.
GIT_COMMIT=

# Frontend Vite dev server port (read from this root file by frontend/vite.config.ts).
FRONTEND_DEV_PORT=5173

# Address the Vite dev server listens on (default: 127.0.0.1, reachable only from this machine).
# Leave it unset unless you want to open ApiPilot from another device on a network you trust.
# Any non-loopback value (0.0.0.0 for every interface, or a specific address) makes a per-launch
# access token mandatory: it is generated at every start, printed once in the terminal as an address to
# open, and required on every request (scripts send it in an X-ApiPilot-Token header). There is no
# setting that turns the token off. The connection is plain HTTP, so anyone who can read traffic on
# that network can read the token; prefer an SSH or VPN tunnel on an untrusted network. The backend
# stays loopback-only either way. See docs/configuration.md ("Opening ApiPilot from another device").
# FRONTEND_DEV_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Debug-only: log the real browser IP (via the Vite dev proxy's X-Forwarded-For header) instead
# of the proxy's own loopback address in request-completion diagnostics (backend/src/app.ts).
# Default: false. Leave off unless actively debugging a client-IP issue locally — never enable
# this in production without also restricting Express's "trust proxy" setting to your actual
# reverse proxy, or a client could spoof its own logged IP.
DEBUG_LOG_REAL_CLIENT_IP=false

# AI provider mode: "local" (Transformers.js) or "mock" (deterministic, no real model).
# Defaults to "mock" during automated tests and "local" otherwise if unset.
AI_PROVIDER_MODE=local

# Hugging Face repo id of the local model to load (default: onnx-community/Qwen3-1.7B-ONNX).
# The default needs about 7 GB of disk and about 10 GB of free memory, and answers a scenario-
# enhancement request in roughly 30 seconds on CPU. onnx-community/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct (about 2 GB
# of disk, 3 GB of memory, 5 to 10 seconds) is the small alternative, but it scored 0% on the
# enhancement benchmark, so scenario enhancement will then mostly come back empty
# (specs/013-ai-enhancement-viability research.md Decision 11).
AI_MODEL_ID=onnx-community/Qwen3-1.7B-ONNX

# Local filesystem cache directory for downloaded model files (default: ~/.apipilot/models).
# A relative path resolves from the backend's working directory, which is backend/ under the npm
# scripts, so "models" means backend/models (git-ignored).
AI_MODEL_CACHE_DIR=models

# Local SQLite database file for the backend's persistence layer (environments/credentials,
# execution run history, AI readiness/benchmark diagnostics — specs/025-local-persistence-layer).
# Default: ~/.apipilot/apipilot.db. A relative path resolves from the backend's working directory
# (backend/ under the npm scripts), so "db/apipilot.db" means backend/db/apipilot.db (git-ignored).
# A sibling key file (<db path>.key, here apipilot.db.key) is generated alongside it on first run
# to encrypt credential-like environment values at rest; back up both files together or neither
# will decrypt.
APIPILOT_DB_PATH=db/apipilot.db

# Optional: the 32-byte credential encryption key, as 64 hex characters or base64
# (generate one with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))").
# When set it takes precedence over the sibling key file, and no key file is read or created, so
# the key stays off the data directory. Unset (default) keeps the file key, whose 0o600 mode has
# no effect on Windows and only protects a copied DB file. A malformed value fails startup. Keep
# it stable: changing it makes previously stored encrypted values undecryptable.
APIPILOT_KEY=

# ONNX weight quantization to load (fp32, fp16, q8, int8, uint8, q4, q4f16, bnb4).
# LEAVE UNSET on CPU. Transformers.js then uses fp32, which measured faster than q8 on the
# reference CPU profile (Qwen2.5-0.5B: ~4x; Qwen3-1.7B, the default: 29 s against 44 s per operation)
# and produced usable scenario candidates where q8 did not (Qwen3-1.7B: 5 of 6 operations against 0 of 6).
# q8 roughly halves memory (4.5 GB against 8.9 GB) but that saving is a false economy here.
# Set a quantization only for a model too large to run unquantized, and measure before trusting it
# (specs/013-ai-enhancement-viability research.md Decision 5).
AI_MODEL_DTYPE=

# Per-request inference timeout in milliseconds (default: 120000). Also the budget for one AI
# failure-analysis explanation (specs/030-ai-failure-analysis).
#
# DELIBERATELY LEFT UNSET so backend/src/ai/modelConfig.ts stays the single source of truth, which
# carries the arithmetic: 256 output tokens at the seeded decode rate cost ~46s before a single
# prompt token is read, and a real 39-operation specification's units measured 430-834 prompt tokens
# on top of that, so every unit projects at 64-81s. The previous 60000 made the feature
# arithmetically impossible — the largest prompts hit the wall (TIMEOUT) and the near-boundary ones
# came back cut off mid-document (INVALID_RESPONSE).
#
# Raising this does NOT make impossible work possible: the pre-flight check still refuses anything
# projected beyond the limit, and AI_ENHANCEMENT_RUN_BUDGET_MS still caps the whole run. It makes
# *possible* work actually fit. Lower it only alongside a smaller output allowance or smaller
# prompts.
AI_INFERENCE_TIMEOUT_MS=

# Conservative context window (tokens) assumed when a model reports none (default: 2048). Lower is
# safer: too low only costs extra batches, too high risks an opaque failure inside the model.
# AI_MODEL_CONTEXT_FLOOR_TOKENS=2048

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tuning knobs below are commented out or left empty so backend/src/ai/modelConfig.ts stays the
# single source of truth for their defaults; a pinned copy here drifts from the code. Uncomment
# one only to override it, ideally from a measurement.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Operations per AI request for scenario enhancement (default: 1).
#
# One, because that is what measurement supports: across a real springdoc-style specification, one
# operation per request produced a validly shaped reply for every operation, while two and three
# operations both truncated mid-document even at a larger output allowance (specs/014-ai-batching-
# policy research.md Decision 1). Context-based splitting remains an upper safety bound. Raise it
# only alongside real measurement on faster hardware or a stronger model — not as a throughput
# shortcut, which is what regressed this default to 2 without evidence until Phase 8 caught it.
# AI_ENHANCEMENT_OPERATIONS_PER_UNIT=1

# Wall-clock ceiling for one whole enhancement run, in milliseconds (default: 300000).
#
# Distinct from AI_INFERENCE_TIMEOUT_MS, which bounds a SINGLE request. One operation per unit makes
# total run time linear in specification size, so a large specification needs a bound of its own:
# without one, a 39-operation specification runs for roughly half an hour. On reaching the ceiling
# the run starts no further units, records the remainder as not attempted, settles `partial`, and
# keeps every scenario it produced.
#
# At the measured ~30-60s per unit the 5-minute default covers roughly 5-10 operations. Raise it if
# you want a large specification covered in one pass and are willing to wait — 39 operations needs
# roughly 40 minutes (for example 2700000 in your own .env).
AI_ENHANCEMENT_RUN_BUDGET_MS=

# Operations per AI request for dependency analysis (default: 3).
#
# Unlike enhancement, this pass needs several operations in view at once to find a relationship
# between them, so the smallest unit is not the best unit here. But an unbounded unit is what let a
# 51-operation specification build one ~20,500-token prompt that crashed the local runtime's memory
# allocator outright (a native "bad allocation", not a graceful input-size refusal). Raise it only
# alongside real measurement of what this machine and model can prefill/decode within
# the per-request AI_DEPENDENCY_TIMEOUT_MS constant (backend/src/dependencies/aiDependencyPrompt.ts,
# fixed in code, not an environment variable) — see
# specs/014-ai-batching-policy research.md Decision 7.
# AI_DEPENDENCY_OPERATIONS_PER_UNIT=3

# Wall-clock ceiling for one whole dependency-analysis AI-assisted pass, in milliseconds
# (default: 120000).
#
# Distinct from the analysis performance budget (which governs only deterministic matching and
# workflow assembly) and from the per-request AI_DEPENDENCY_TIMEOUT_MS (50000, a constant in
# backend/src/dependencies/aiDependencyPrompt.ts, not an environment variable — the original 8000
# predated the prompt projection and was never actually achievable on the reference hardware). Two minutes matches the "this can
# take a couple of minutes" copy already shown while finalizing scenario review, and covers roughly
# two full-cost requests at the 50s per-request budget. On reaching the ceiling, remaining units are
# recorded as not attempted and the pass settles `partial`, keeping whatever it found.
# AI_DEPENDENCY_RUN_BUDGET_MS=120000

# Seed throughput estimates for the pre-flight viability check (ms/token) and the margin by which a
# projection may exceed the per-request timeout before a run is refused.
#
# DELIBERATELY LEFT UNSET so backend/src/ai/modelConfig.ts stays the single source of truth. These
# were previously pinned here at 42 / 130 / 1.5 while the code defaults moved to 42 / 180 / 1.0, and
# the stale safety factor silently disabled the pre-flight refusal it was meant to configure: work
# projected at 68s was admitted against a 60s hard timeout because 68 <= 60 x 1.5. Every unit then
# failed, one at a time, for the whole run.
#
# Set them only to override the code, and only from a measurement on a quiescent machine. PREFILL
# DOMINATES: ~42 ms per prompt token on the reference CPU profile, linear in prompt size (43.4 /
# 41.3 / 42.1 ms/token at 441 / 828 / 1,132 prompt tokens). Keep the safety factor at or below 1.0 —
# above it, the check approves work the timeout is guaranteed to kill.
AI_PREFILL_MS_PER_TOKEN=
AI_DECODE_MS_PER_TOKEN=
AI_VIABILITY_SAFETY_FACTOR=

# EXPERIMENTAL. Whether to attempt hardware-accelerated inference (default: false; CPU is the
# recommended setting). It was measured slower than CPU for the models ApiPilot ships: on 2026-10-17,
# on one Windows laptop (Intel integrated graphics plus an NVIDIA RTX 500 Ada), Qwen2.5-0.5B was
# correct but 2x to 4x slower than CPU on real enhancement prompts, and Qwen3-1.7B (the default)
# returned unusable output on DirectML at fp32 and fp16 on both GPUs. Other machines may differ.
#
# So an enabled accelerator is verified after it loads: one small fixed check inference must return a
# usable reply. If the accelerator fails to load, or loads but fails that check, ApiPilot falls back
# to CPU and says so (never to another provider). GET /api/ai/status shows the device in use
# (inferenceDevice), the reason for a fallback (reason), and the check's result with a coarse
# throughput sample (acceleratorVerification); reference CPU rates are in docs/configuration.md.
#
# On Windows this requests DirectML specifically (not Transformers.js's generic "gpu" device,
# which resolves on Windows to an execution-provider combination onnxruntime-node's DirectML
# backend rejects outright, always throwing and falling back to CPU regardless of real GPU
# availability — see backend/src/ai/localProvider.ts's resolveAcceleratorDevice() and
# specs/004-ai-provider-local-inference/research.md Decision 6's addendum). On Linux and macOS the
# library resolves its generic GPU request itself, which ApiPilot has not measured, so do not assume
# a specific accelerator there. After enabling this, check GET /api/ai/status (acceleratorActive and
# acceleratorVerification) to confirm what actually runs on your machine, rather than assuming.
AI_USE_ACCELERATOR=false

# k6 performance testing (AP-029, specs/031-k6-performance-testing). OPTIONAL.
#
# The path to a k6 binary you installed yourself, version 1.0.0 or later. ApiPilot never
# downloads, bundles or installs k6. When unset, the first `k6` (`k6.exe` on Windows) on PATH is
# used. Performance runs are unavailable, with the reason shown, until a usable k6 is found; the
# generated script can still be downloaded and run elsewhere.
# K6_BINARY_PATH=

# Input limits (AP-056, specs/056-har-json-input-support). OPTIONAL.
#
# Limits the guided workflow's upload applies to HAR captures and JSON files, on top of the existing
# 10 MiB upload size limit (which is not configurable). Each must be a positive integer; a bad value
# stops the backend at startup. Input beyond a limit is refused, or (for one body) skipped and
# reported, with a message that names the limit.
#
# Most entries a HAR may hold before it is refused. Capture a narrower session if you reach it.
# INPUT_HAR_MAX_ENTRIES=5000
#
# Largest request or response body, in bytes, that is analyzed. A larger body is skipped and reported.
# INPUT_MAX_BODY_BYTES=1048576
#
# Deepest container (object or array) nesting a JSON document may have before it is refused.
# INPUT_MAX_JSON_DEPTH=128
